{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfwWamjS36ecJDFR4YfmP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana or watched the rule run, so I don't know yet whether the panels render or the rule fires correctly.\n\n**Data:** The logs data view uses `timestamp` as its time field. The fields I used are:\n- `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as text)\n- `bytes`\n- `extension`\n- `clientip`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-ea71af4d Web logs overview\" has four panels, with a default time range of the last 7 days.\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Requests by response code\n- Average bytes by file extension\n\nLink: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-ea71af4d-web-logs-overview-ade623692bd1\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-ea71af4d 5xx spike\" (ID `fdbbe3a9-a7da-48d0-8da6-e10b291e202a`) runs every minute. It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It counts documents whose `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]` over a 5-minute window and fires when the count is above 10.\n\nThe rule has no notification action attached, so a firing only shows up in Kibana's alert list. I can add a connector action if you want one."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":165,"cache_read_input_tokens":20333,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":165},"output_tokens":474,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4eb1cb7-866c-5bbf-8d81-a8d5acd83be3","uuid":"4f69013e-1ece-41f3-a993-ccb4cd008459","timestamp":"2026-10-12T01:55:28.629Z","request_id":"req_011CfwWamXnXoHzCxsBhit13"}
